sound, the standard practice of fixing the simulator and environment during interactive-agent RL training should be replaced by adaptive co-evolution.\n  * Meta-agent-guided adaptation could reduce the manual effort of debugging simulators, reward functions, and hyperparameters for multi-turn agent tasks.\n  * The observed gains (up to 36.7% on ColBench) suggest that training-system adaptation may be as important as policy optimization in achieving high performance.\n  * The method is compatible with multiple RL algorithms (PPO, UserRL, InfoPO) and meta-agent backbones (Claude Opus, Codex), indicating broad applicability.\n  * Performance improvements transfer to held-out environments (e.g., Inten. in UserGym), suggesting that co-evolution fosters generalizable agent behaviors.\n\nAcross three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\nFigures\n\nImage: Figure 1 Figure 1. Illustration of how our meta-agent, EAU, manages RL training runs. EAU surpasses the prior SOTA on ColBench. Left: For the first three runs, EAU proactively restarts when it identifies issues that can only be resolved with offline patches. Middle: Run 4 and 5 displayed in blue and red are two full runs. Dashed lines indicate online, intra-run modifications. Run 4 explored individual modifications through local perturbations, whereas Run 5 used endpoint interventions followed by replication, reversal, and interaction tests. 1 arXiv:2610.16523v1 [cs.AI] 12 Oct 2026 Image: Figure 2 Figure 2. Illustration of a user￾centric agentic task. Thus, we have Ttool : (stool, atool) 7→ (s ′ tool, ztool) and Tuser : (suser,… Image: Figure 4 Figure 4. Illustration of symbolic user simulator. Image: Figure 5 Figure 5